{'PATENTS_GOOGLE': {'PATENTS_GOOGLE': ['CPC_DEFINITION', 'DISCLOSURES_13', 'PUBLICATIONS', 'ABS_AND_EMB']}}
Begin Exploring Related Columns
Relevant tables / columns and why they matter  

• PATENTS_GOOGLE.PATENTS_GOOGLE.PUBLICATIONS  
  – "family_id": simple‑family identifier – grouping key  
  – "publication_date": earliest (min) date per family; filter to 20150101‑20150131  
  – "publication_number": list of all publications we finally need  
  – "country_code": country of each publication  
  – "cpc": VARIANT array of dictionaries; we must FLATTEN and pick the code field → distinct CPC codes  
  – "ipc": VARIANT array of dictionaries; likewise → distinct IPC codes  
  – "citation": VARIANT array that contains publications / families this doc cites; after FLATTEN we can collect cited family_ids  
• (same table again) PUBLICATIONS through a self‑join – to find “citing families”: any other publication whose "citation" list contains one of our family’s publication_numbers or family_ids  

Conditions that will be useful  

1. publication_date BETWEEN 20150101 AND 20150131  
2. "publication_date" = (SELECT MIN(publication_date) … GROUP BY family_id) to get the earliest pub per family  
3. FLATTEN(input => "cpc") and FLATTEN(input => "ipc") to extract the code fields, e.g. f.value:"code"  
4. FLATTEN(input => "citation") to pull out c.value:"family_id" or c.value:"number"  
5. Self‑join on citation values to discover reverse citations (families that cite our family)  

Below are 10 progressively richer Snowflake SQL queries (each limited to 20 rows) that let you explore the problem space and build up to the final answer.  
(Remember to run them in order to understand the data structure in your environment.)

```sql
-- 1. Look at raw rows in January‑2015 to understand date format & fields
SELECT 
    "family_id",
    "publication_number",
    "publication_date",
    "country_code",
    "cpc",
    "ipc",
    "citation"
FROM PATENTS_GOOGLE.PATENTS_GOOGLE.PUBLICATIONS
WHERE "publication_date" BETWEEN 20150101 AND 20150131
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 2. Get the earliest publication per family in Jan‑2015
SELECT 
    "family_id",
    MIN("publication_date") AS "earliest_pub_date"
FROM PATENTS_GOOGLE.PATENTS_GOOGLE.PUBLICATIONS
WHERE "publication_date" BETWEEN 20150101 AND 20150131
GROUP BY "family_id"
ORDER BY "earliest_pub_date"
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 3. List all publication_numbers for one sample family (replace 'YOUR_FAMILY_ID')
SELECT 
    "family_id",
    "publication_number",
    "country_code",
    "publication_date"
FROM PATENTS_GOOGLE.PATENTS_GOOGLE.PUBLICATIONS
WHERE "family_id" = 'YOUR_FAMILY_ID'
ORDER BY "publication_number"
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 4. Inspect CPC array structure for a sample publication
SELECT 
    "publication_number",
    f.value AS "cpc_raw"
FROM PATENTS_GOOGLE.PATENTS_GOOGLE.PUBLICATIONS p,
     LATERAL FLATTEN(input => p."cpc") f
WHERE p."publication_number" = 'YOUR_PUBLICATION_NUMBER'
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 5. Extract distinct CPC codes for a sample family
SELECT DISTINCT
    f.value:"code"::STRING AS "cpc_code"
FROM PATENTS_GOOGLE.PATENTS_GOOGLE.PUBLICATIONS p,
